      "id": "SP-01",
      "found_during": "wave 1 review (fe6fa7e5)",
      "subject": "Dangling path citations in the epic's own planning documents",
      "detail": "DOCS_FOOTPRINT=dev/active/f1dc843a-redo-mutations ./scripts/docs-mechanical.sh reports 4 MISSING citations on clean main at 60bab4d6f: crates/jit/benches, dev/benchmarks/test-suite-performance-4b7c06d0.json, .jit/.gitignore, .jit/tmp/transactions. Pre-existing, not worker-introduced (verified against clean main). The docs-mechanical gate's footprint is docs/, so these never block a task gate.",
      "disposition": "RESOLVED to owners (wave 2 analysis), none blocking. (1) .jit/.gitignore -- created by 08b8c0ef (wave 7), citation resolves then. (2) .jit/tmp/transactions -- the retired kernel path; goes with b0ee2dd6 kernel-retirement (wave 12). (3) crates/jit/benches -- investigation.md:2050 finding F-16 cites it AS ABSENT ('crates/jit/benches absent') to evidence that no Rust benchmark harness exists. The prose is correct and the path's absence is its point; the M3 checker cannot tell a citation-of-absence from a dangling one. No action. (4) dev/benchmarks/test-suite-performance-4b7c06d0.json -- ARCHIVED to dev/archive/4b7c06d0-test-suite-performance/benchmarks/. Still cited live by dev/TESTING.md:471, which is permanent_paths living documentation, alongside the contract name `transaction-benchmark-evidence` that plan decision LD-4 retires with the journal. OWNER: 8b6a9e34 (wave 22, Testing guide publication-cost evidence). Verify both the path and the contract name are gone there.",
      "criterion_subjects": [
        "REQ-07"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SP-02",
      "found_during": "wave 1 (fe6fa7e5 worker flag, lead-verified)",
      "subject": "Inconsistent capture byte budgets cap event-log size; jit claim acquire fails above 16 MiB",
      "detail": "commands/claim.rs:191-194 sets CaptureBudget.max_bytes = 16 MiB on a CaptureSpec::phase_one that includes VirtualPath::EVENTS, so `jit claim acquire` errors ('captured 33519044 bytes but the maximum is 16777216') on the epic's own 10x benchmark fixture. commands/mod.rs:2186-2191 gives the issue-update path 64 MiB, which is why the label-update scenarios still run. No task in the breakdown names either constant. c88822a5 REQ-03 keeps the event log in the single-pass base specification and REQ-06 keeps capture budget-bounded, so the ceiling survives the retirement phase unless a task takes it. REFINED (wave 2): the whole-log PARSE (captured_events, mutation.rs:695, called only at :884 under MutationIntent::ClaimIssue) is claim-specific, so a label update never pays it and the benchmark medians stand. Two consequences split apart. (a) The claim ceiling is a real latent product bug -- `jit claim acquire` fails above 16 MiB -- but it is NOT on the path REQ-01/REQ-02 evidence measures. (b) The residual REQ-02 risk is different: the finalizer's whole-log copy (mutation.rs:1224, `.to_vec()` + compose_events) is addressed by b4483ad6 positioned-event-append, but capture still READS the log whole into the image, which c88822a5 REQ-03 explicitly preserves. A 33 MB read is roughly 10-15 ms against a ~100 ms budget, i.e. at or over REQ-02's 10 % margin, with no parse cost to remove.",
      "disposition": "OPEN, split. (a) claim 16 MiB ceiling: create a remediation task inside the epic; it blocks no criterion but is an adjacent latent bug the epic surfaced. (b) REQ-02 margin from the whole-log read: this is the one that can fail a criterion. Decide at wave 16 (c88822a5) whether the single-pass base specification can take the log as length+tail rather than whole bytes, given the only whole-log consumer left is the claim path. Re-measure at wave 21 (722878d4) before accepting REQ-02.",
      "criterion_subjects": [
        "REQ-02",
        "REQ-05"
      ]
    }
  ],
